---
description: APPLY smart contract testing best practices WHEN writing Hardhat tests. Covers fixtures with loadFixture, edge cases, fuzz/property testing, invariants, event assertions, revert reasons, custom errors, reentrancy testing, fork testing patterns, and gas regression testing.
globs: test/**/*.ts, test/**/*.js, **/*.test.ts, **/*.spec.ts
alwaysApply: false
---

Fixtures and Setup:
- Always use loadFixture for test setup
- Create reusable fixture functions
- Fixtures should represent realistic protocol states
- Test both happy-path and edge-case scenarios in fixtures
- Avoid shared mutable state between tests
- Use helpers for common deployment patterns
- Keep fixtures focused and composable

Edge Cases:
- Test zero values and empty inputs
- Test maximum values and overflow boundaries
- Test boundary conditions thoroughly
- Test invalid inputs and revert conditions
- Test state transitions exhaustively
- Test concurrent operations when relevant

Fuzz and Property Testing:
- Use fuzzing for input validation functions
- Test invariants with random inputs
- Use property-based tests for arithmetic operations
- Explicitly state the invariant being tested
- Document invariants in comments or helper functions
- Fuzz with realistic value ranges
- Test with edge case generators when possible
- Document fuzzing coverage goals

Invariants:
- Assert invariants after every state change
- Test that invariants hold across operations
- Use invariant checks in helper functions
- Verify accounting invariants explicitly
- Test invariant violations fail appropriately
- Document critical invariants in test comments

Event Assertions:
- Assert all events are emitted correctly
- Verify event parameters match expectations
- Test event emission order when relevant
- Use event filters for complex assertions
- Assert event count matches expectations
- Test events in integration scenarios

Revert Reasons and Custom Errors:
- Test that functions revert with correct errors
- Assert custom error types explicitly
- Test revert conditions comprehensively
- Verify error messages are appropriate
- Test nested revert scenarios
- Use expectRevert or expectCustomError helpers

Reentrancy and Security Testing:
- Test reentrancy attempts explicitly
- Use malicious mock contracts to simulate attacks
- Test callback and hook patterns (ERC777-style)
- Verify reentrancy guards work correctly
- Test cross-function reentrancy scenarios
- Simulate adversarial external contracts
- Test access control bypass attempts
- Verify emergency pause mechanisms

Fork Testing:
- Use fork tests for integration with mainnet contracts
- Test against real contract addresses when needed
- Use fork tests for complex protocol interactions
- Pin block numbers for determinism
- Reset fork state between tests
- Fork tests are integration-level, not unit-level
- Document fork test requirements clearly
- Consider gas costs in fork tests

Test Organization:
- Group related tests using describe blocks
- Use descriptive test names in English
- Follow Arrange-Act-Assert pattern
- Keep tests independent and isolated
- Test one behavior per test case
- Use setup helpers to reduce duplication

Coverage and Quality:
- Aim for high coverage of critical paths
- Test both happy paths and failure paths
- Test access control thoroughly
- Verify state changes explicitly
- Test reentrancy scenarios when applicable
- Test upgrade paths if upgradeable

Gas and Performance:
- Gas tests are for regression detection
- Avoid premature optimization in tests
- Test gas optimization scenarios when relevant
- Verify gas limits are not exceeded
- Test batch operations for efficiency
- Document gas expectations in comments
